10 points, with the most recent survey at 45-35", "Prediction markets price Democratic chances at only 5-6 percent", "Expert ratings uniformly favor Republicans, from Likely R to Safe R"], "rationale": "The state's Republican lean, a polling average near 10 points for Wilson, and markets giving Johnson roughly a 5 percent chance all point to a comfortable but not landslide Republican win. Ratings consensus of Safe R is slightly ahead of the polling evidence, so Likely R fits best.", "watch": "A late polling shift or a strong Johnson debate/fundraising surge could tighten the race, but no current evidence suggests movement.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Henry McMaster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's open governor's race pits Republican Attorney General Alan Wilson against Democratic state Representative Jermaine Johnson, with term-limited Henry McMaster not on the ballot. The state's Republican fundamentals give Wilson a clear edge, and the polling that exists backs that up: a recent October survey shows him ahead 45-35, and earlier polls from the summer had similar margins. Prediction markets give Johnson only about a 5 percent chance of winning.\n\nExpert raters lean even more bullish on Republicans, with most calling the race Solid or Safe R. The debate coverage in early October shows a competitive exchange on taxes, data centers, and Medicaid, but no sign of a race-altering moment. The forecast expects Wilson to win by roughly nine or ten points, with Johnson's path running through a major late shift in the campaign that has not yet appeared.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0588, "margin": -9.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0546, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0722, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0597, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0544, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0532, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p